---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Policy from '@/components/motion/scenes/Policy.astro';
import { PolicyControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/PolicyControls';
// The no-JavaScript demonstration: its labels and its swatch are what the browser test reads, so keep them as they are.
const example = `<div class="example">
  <input id="reduce" type="checkbox">
  <label for="reduce">Reduce motion in this demonstration</label>
  <input id="toggle" type="checkbox">
  <label for="toggle">Change the decorative swatch</label>
  <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
</div>
<style>
  .swatch { opacity: .35; transition: opacity
    calc(300ms * var(--udx-motion-scale, 1)); }
  #toggle:checked ~ .swatch { opacity: 1; }
  #reduce:checked ~ .swatch { transition-duration: 0s; }
</style>`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="accessibility"
  title="Motion: reduced motion and accessibility"
  description="The reduced-motion policy of @udixio/motion, what each verb shows under it, and the focus, pause and reading rules that the runtime keeps."
>
  <MotionStage scene="policy" label="Live example: Reduced motion" height={[256, 272]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><PolicyControls client:visible /></Fragment>
    <Policy />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>
    The runtime follows the reduced-motion policy of the document. The system preference <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>, or
    <code>&lt;html data-motion-policy="reduce"&gt;</code>, selects it. An absent attribute, <code>auto</code>, or an unknown value follows
    the system preference. The document stylesheet defines <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> as 1 by default, and as 0 under the reduced policy.
    The Reduce motion button of the navigation rail writes the attribute for the whole documentation site. The stage above reads the scale and says
    what it changes.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`<html data-motion-policy="reduce">`}</code></pre>
  <p>A single element can keep its end state, whatever the policy: a class of <code>--udx-motion: none</code> does it.</p>
  <pre><code>{`<div data-motion="slide" class="[--udx-motion:none]">…</div>`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The examples keep a card's presence and send its focus to the toggle when it leaves, and show an element that stays still. Each
    framework names the restore target of the exit.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-policy" live={false} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'reduced', values: 'fade · instant · static · neutral · frozen', default: 'by verb: fade, instant, static, neutral or frozen', description: 'What the motion shows under reduced motion, named in the definition.' },
      { name: '--udx-motion', values: 'none · a defined motion of the same kind', default: 'the motion of the element', description: 'The motion an element plays in place of its own. none holds the end state, whatever the policy.' },
      { name: 'data-motion-policy', values: 'reduce · auto', default: 'auto', description: 'On html: reduce selects the reduced policy; auto, an absent attribute or an unknown value follows the system.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The default springs time an entry. Under the policy, an entry is a short fade, and the document scale multiplies its duration.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>What each verb shows under reduced motion:</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Under reduced motion</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>appear</code>, <code>disappear</code></td><td>A short opacity fade. An exit with <code>exit: 'out-of-flow'</code> is in place.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>emphasize</code></td><td>Not animated: the element applies its end frame.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>reveal</code></td><td>The element is shown at once, unless its definition sets another <code>reduced</code> value.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scrub</code></td><td>The element stays still; the scroll link is stopped.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loop</code></td><td>The loop does not play: the element keeps its stylesheet state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>layout</code></td><td>The new layout applies at once.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gestures</td><td>The release is written at once, with no glide.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Page transitions</td><td><code>navigate()</code> runs the update alone.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Text</td><td>The text is not split: the element plays whole.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scroll</td><td><code>scrollTo</code> jumps to its destination.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Values</td><td>A smoothed value is written at once; a <code>time</code> value holds the first end of its range.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p><code>--udx-motion: none</code> on an element or an ancestor applies the end state without animating, whatever the policy. Use it per breakpoint with Tailwind: <code>md:[--udx-motion:none]</code>.</p>
  <p>Multiplying a transition duration by <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> alone does not make every effect safe. Adapt or remove translation and loops as appropriate, and keep essential content available. A raw provider does not apply this policy.</p>

  <h2>Focus when an element leaves (WCAG 2.4.3)</h2>
  <p>An exit sets <code>inert</code> on its element. When the exit starts with focus inside it, <code>restoreFocus</code>, or <code>data-motion-restore-focus="&lt;selector&gt;"</code> in HTML, names the element that takes focus first. See <a href="/motion/presence">Show and hide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Pausing a loop (WCAG 2.2.2)</h2>
  <p>A loop never ends, so each loop states in its definition how it is paused: <code>pause: 'control'</code> when the page offers a control, or <code>pause: 'essential'</code> with a reason when the loop is the content itself. See <a href="/motion/loop">Loop, and pause it</a>.</p>

  <h2>Reading and keyboard</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A counter holds its number in an <code>aria-hidden</code> span while it counts, and the final number in a visually hidden span, so a screen reader reads the final number.</li>
    <li>A split text keeps its semantics, and its visual copy is <code>aria-hidden</code>: the text is read once, as written (see <a href="/motion/text">Animate text</a>).</li>
    <li>Every gesture has a keyboard alternative, and a reorder announces each step in a live region (see <a href="/motion/gestures">Drag, swipe, reorder</a>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it without JavaScript</h2>
  <p>The checkbox state remains visible and the final appearance is available without animation. The reduction checkbox only changes this demonstration, not your system or document policy.</p>
  <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] items-center gap-3 rounded-2xl border border-current p-6">
    <input id="demo-reduce" type="checkbox" class="peer/reduce size-5 accent-primary focus-visible:outline-3 focus-visible:outline-current focus-visible:outline-offset-3" /> <label for="demo-reduce">Reduce motion in this demonstration</label>
    <input id="demo-toggle" type="checkbox" class="peer/toggle size-5 accent-primary focus-visible:outline-3 focus-visible:outline-current focus-visible:outline-offset-3" /> <label for="demo-toggle">Change the decorative swatch</label>
    <span class="swatch col-span-full h-12 w-20 rounded-xl bg-primary opacity-35 ease-[ease] transition-[opacity] duration-[calc(300ms*var(--udx-motion-scale,1))] peer-checked/toggle:opacity-100 peer-checked/reduce:duration-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>
  <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>

  <h2>Shadow DOM</h2>
  <p>The HTML selector does not cross a shadow boundary. The scale set by the document stylesheet can be inherited into a shadow root, but a local override can defeat it. Without the document stylesheet, the HTML attribute alone does not guarantee reduction inside a shadow root.</p>
  <p>Search has its own behavior: it respects the system preference even without the stylesheet, has a 200 ms and easing fallback, and reads the attribute in ordinary DOM. Do not generalize this behavior to all components or providers.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Inside a shadow root the attribute alone does not reach: the document stylesheet is needed (see Shadow DOM above).</li>
    <li>The policy applies to the runtime and its adapters; a raw provider does not apply it.</li>
  </ul>

</MotionPage>
